° MORI  
 
by 
 
Jonathan Nolan  
 
 
What like a bullet can undeceive!"  
                         —Herman Melville  
 
Your wife always used to  say you'd be late for your own funeral. 
Remember that? Her little joke because you were such a slob —always 
late, always forgetting stuff, even before the incident.  
 
Right about now you're probably wondering if you were late for hers.  
 
You were there, yo u can be sure of that. That's what ═'s for —
the one tacked æ wall by ő. It's not customary to take 
pictures at a funeral, but somebody, your doctors, I guess, knew you 
wouldn't remember. They had it blown up nice and big and stuck it r ight 
there, next to ő, so you couldn't help but see it every time you 
got up to find out where she was.  
 
The ü in ═, the one ắ flowers? That's you. And what 
are you doing? You're reading the headstone, trying to figure out who's 
funeral you're at, same as you're reading it now, trying to figure why 
someone stuck that picture next to your door. But why bother reading 
something that you won't remember?  
 
She's gone, gone for good, and you must be hurting right now, hearing 
the news.  Believe me, ₁ how you feel. You're probably a wreck. But 
give it five minutes, maybe ten. Maybe you can even go a whole half 
hour before you forget.  
 
But you will forget —I guarantee it. A few more minutes and you'll be 
heading for ő, looking for her all over again, breaking down 
when you find ═. How many times ■ ♀ hear the news 
before some other part of your body, other than that busted brain of 
yours, starts to remember?  
 
 Never-ending grief, never -ending anger. Useless w ithout direction. 
Maybe you can't understand what's happened. Can't say I really 
understand, either. Backwards amnesia. That's whå sign says. CRS 
disease. Your guess is as good as mine.  
 
Maybe you can't understand what happened to you. But you do rem ember 
what happened to HER, don't you? The doctors don't ★ talk about 
it. They won't answer my questions. They don't think it's right for a 
man in your condition to hear about those things. But you remember 
enough, don't you? You remember his face.  
 
This is why I'm writing to you. Futile, maybe. I don't know how many 
times you'll ♀ read this before you listen to me. I don't even 
know how long you've been locked up in this room already. Neither do 
you. But your advantage in forgetting is that yo u'll forget to write 
ợ off as a lost cause.  
 
Sooner or later you'll ★ do something about it. And when you do, 
you'll just ♀ trust me, because I'm the only one who can help 
you. 
 
______________________________   
 
EARL OPENS ONE EYE after another to a stretch of white ceiling tiles 
interrupted by a hand -printed sign taped right above ●, large 
enough for him to read ě bed. An alarm clock is ringing 
somewhere. He reads the sign, blinks,reads it again, then takes a look 
å ro om.  
 
It's a white room, overwhelmingly white, ě walls ™ 
curtains æ institutional furniture and ổspread. The alarm 
clock is ringing ě white desk under the window ắ white 
curtains. At this point č probably notices th at he is lying on top 
ă white comforter. He is already wearing a dressing gown and 
slippers.  
 
He lies back and reads the sign taped æ ceiling again. It says, in 
crude block capitals, THIS IS YOUR ROOM. THIS IS A ROOM IN A HOSPITAL. 
THIS IS WHERE  YOU LIVE NOW.  
 
č rises and takes a look around. The room is large for a hospital —
empty linoleum stretches out ě bed in three directions. Two 
doors and a window. The view isn't very helpful, either —a close of 
trees ä center of a carefully m anicured piece of turf that 
 terminates in a sliver of two -lane blacktop. The trees, except ź 
evergreens, are bare —early spring or late fall, one or the other.  
 
Every inch Î desk is covered with Post -it notes, legal pads, 
neatly printed lists, p sychological textbooks, framed pictures. On top 
Î mess is a half -completed crossword puzzle. The alarm clock is 
riding a pile of folded newspapers. č slaps the snooze button and 
takes a cigarette ě pack taped æ sleeve ă dressing 
gown. He pats the empty İs ă pajamas for a light. He rifles 
the papers É desk, looks quickly ậ drawers. Eventually 
he finds a box of kitchen matches taped æ wall next æ window. 
Another sign is taped just above the box. It s ays in loud yellow 
letters, CIGARETTE? CHECK FOR LIT ONES FIRST, STUPID.  
 
č laughs å sign, lights his cigarette, and takes a long draw. 
Taped æ window in front of him is another piece of looseleaf paper 
headed YOUR SCHEDULE.  
 
It charts off t he hours, every hour, in blocks: 10:00 p.m. to 8:00 a.m. 
is labeled go BACK TO SLEEP. č consults the alarm clock: 8:15. Given 
the light outside, it must be morning. He checks his watch: 10:30. He 
presses the watch to his ear and ở. He gives the wa tch a wind or 
two and sets it to match the alarm clock.  
 
According æ schedule, the entire block from 8:00 to 8:30 has been 
labeled BRUSH YOUR TEETH. č laughs again and walks over æ 
bathroom.  
 
The bathroom window is open. As he flaps his arms  to keep warm, he 
notices the ashtray É windowsill. A cigarette is perched É 
ashtray, burning steadily through a long finger of ash. He frowns, 
extinguishes the old butt, and replaces it ắ new one.  
 
The toothbrush has already been treated  to a smudge of white paste. The 
tap is Î push -button variety —a dose of water with each nudge. č 
pushes the brush ố cheek and fiddles it back and forth while he 
opens the medicine cabinet. The shelves are stocked with single -serving 
packages  of vitamins, aspirin, antidiuretics. The mouthwash is also 
single -serving, about a shot -glass -worth of blue liquid in a sealed 
plastic bottle. Only the toothpaste is regular -sized. č spits the 
paste š mouth and replaces it ắ mouthwash. As he lays 
the toothbrush next æ toothpaste, he notices a tiny wedge of paper 
pinched between the glass shelf ™ steel backing Î medicine 
cabinet. He spits the frothy blue fluid ó sink and nudges for 
some more water to rinse it down. He closes the medicine cabinet and 
ơ at his reflection ä mirror.  
  
"Who needs half an hour to brush their teeth?"  
 
The paper has been folded down to a minuscule size ẩll the 
precision of a sixth -grader's love note. č unfolds it and smoot hs it 
against the mirror. It reads —  
 
IF YOU CAN STILL READ THIS, THEN YOU'RE A FUCKING COWARD.  
 
č │ blankly å paper, then reads it again. He ř it 
over. On ű it reads —  
 
P.S.: AFTER YOU'VE READ THIS, HIDE IT AGAIN.  
 
č reads bot h sides again, then folds the note back down to its 
original size and tucks it underneath the toothpaste.  
 
Maybe then he notices the scar. It begins just beneath the ear, jagged 
and thick, and disappears abruptly ố hairline. č ř his 
head and  │ õ corner ă eye to follow the scar's 
progress. He traces it ẩ fingertip, then looks back down å 
cigarette burning ä ashtray. A thought seizes him and he spins out 
Î bathroom.  
 
He is caught at ő to his room, one hand É knob. Two 
pictures are taped æ wall by ő. č's attention is caught 
first ế MRI, a shiny black frame for four windows into someone's 
skull. In marker, ═ is labeled YOUR BRAIN. č │ at it. 
Concentric circle s in different colors. He can make out the big orbs of 
his eyes and, ọ these, the twin lobes ă brain. Smooth 
wrinkles, circles, semicircles. But right there ä middle ă 
head, circled in marker, tunneled in from ō his neck like a 
maggot into an apricot, is something different. Deformed, broken, but 
unmistakable. A dark smudge, the shape of a flower, right there ä 
middle ă brain.  
 
He bends to look å other picture. It is a photograph of a man 
holding flowers, standi ng over a fresh grave. The man is bent over, 
reading the headstone. For a moment this looks like a hall of mirrors 
or the beginnings of a sketch of infinity: the one man bent over, 
looking å smaller man, bent over, reading the headstone. č 
ń  ═ for a long time. Maybe he begins to cry. Maybe he 
just │ silently at ═. Eventually, he makes his way back 
to ổ, flops down, seals his eyes shut, tries to sleep.  
 
 The cigarette burns steadily away ä bathroom. A circuit  ä 
alarm clock counts down from ten, and it starts ringing again.  
 
č opens one eye after another to a stretch of white ceiling tiles, 
interrupted by a hand -printed sign taped right above ●, large 
enough for him to read ě bed.  
 
______ ________________________  
 
You can't have a normal life anymore. You must know that. How can you 
have a girlfriend if you can't remember her name? Can't have kids, not 
unless you want them to grow up ẩ dad who doesn't recognize them. 
Sure as hell can' t hold down a job. Not too many professions out there 
that value forgetfulness. Prostitution, maybe. Politics, of course.  
 
No. Your life is over. You're a dead man.The only thing the doctors are 
hoping to do is teach you to be less of a burden æ orde rlies. And 
they'll probably never let you go home, wherever that would be.  
 
So the question is not "to be or not to be," because you aren't. The 
question is whether you ★ do something about it. Whether revenge 
matters to you.  
 
It does to most peopl e. For a few weeks, they plot, they scheme, they 
take measures ₂ even. But the passage of time is all it takes to 
erode that initial impulse. Time is theft, isn't that whåy say? 
And time eventually convinces most of us that forgiveness is a virtu e. 
Conveniently, cowardice and forgiveness look identical at a certain 
distance. Time steals your nerve.  
 
If time and fear aren't enough to dissuade people ěir revenge, 
then there's always authority, softly shaking its head and saying, We 
understan d, but you're the better man for letting it go. For rising 
above it. For not sinking æir level. And besides, says authority, 
if you try anything stupid, we'll lock you up in a little room.  
 
But they already put you in a little room, didn't they? Only  they don't 
really lock it or even guard it too carefully because you're a cripple. 
A corpse. A vegetable who probably wouldn't remember to eat or take a 
shit if someone wasn't there to remind you.  
 
 And as ź passage of time, well, that doesn't reall y apply to you 
anymore, does it? Just the same ten minutes, over and over again. So 
how can you forgive if you can't remember to forget?  
 
You probably were the type to let it go, weren't you? Before. But 
you're not the man you used to be. Not even half. Y ou're a fraction; 
you're the ten -minute man.  
 
Of course, weakness is strong. It's the primary impulse. You'd probably 
prefer to sit in your little room and cry. Live in your finite 
collection of memories, carefully polishing each one. Half a life set 
ọ glass and pinned to cardboard like a collection of exotic 
insects. You'd like to live ọ that glass, wouldn't you? Preserved 
in aspic.  
 
You'd like to but you can't, can you? You can't because Î last 
addition to your collection. The last thing you remember. His face. His 
face and your wife, looking to you for help.  
 
And maybe this is where you can retire to when it's over. Your little 
collection. They can lock you back up in another little room and you 
can live the rest of your life ä past . But only if you've got a 
little piece of paper in your hand that says you got him.  
 
You know I'm right. You know there's a lot of work to do. It may seem 
impossible, but I'm sure if we all do our part, we'll figure something 
out. But you don't have much  time. You've only got about ten minutes, 
in fact. Then it starts all over again. So do something ắ time 
you've got.  
 
